/* 英文 · 潮汐學院原創練習題，非大考中心官方試題（單字卡另檔） */
TA.addUnits('english', [

  /* ───────────────────────── 1. 詞彙題 ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-vocab',
    name: '詞彙題',
    notes: [
      { t: '詞彙題三步驟', b: '① 讀完整句，圈出線索字（because、although、so…that、分號、破折號）。<br>② 判斷空格語意是正面還是負面、是原因還是結果。<br>③ 把答案代回去，檢查搭配詞（vulnerable <b>to</b>、skeptical <b>of</b>、compensate A <b>for</b> B）。' },
      { t: '線索字地圖', b: '<b>轉折</b>（although, but, yet）→ 前後語意相反；<b>因果</b>（because, so…that, since）→ 前後一致；<b>同位說明</b>（分號、破折號、that is）→ 後句就是空格的定義，例如「…; the only question is how fast」暗示 inevitable。' },
      { t: '字首判讀', b: 're-（回、再）recede 退去、relieve 減輕；in-/il-/im-/un-（否定）illegible 難辨認、unprecedented 史無前例；de-（向下、去除）deprive 剝奪；under-（在下、暗中）undermine 暗中破壞。' },
      { t: '易混淆字組', b: 'effective 有效的／extensive 廣泛的／excessive 過度的；depict 描繪／deprive 剝奪／detect 偵測；alleviate 減輕／aggravate 惡化；distract 使分心／extract 萃取；adequate 足夠的／accurate 準確的／adjacent 鄰近的。' }
    ],
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-vocab-01', d: 1, q: `Jenny is so ＿＿＿ that she always arrives at least ten minutes early for every appointment.`, o: [`generous`, `punctual`, `curious`, `humble`], a: 1, e: `punctual 守時的，與「總是提早十分鐘到」形成 so…that 的因果。generous 慷慨的、curious 好奇的、humble 謙虛的，都無法解釋「提早到」這個結果。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-02', d: 2, q: `After weeks of heavy rain, the farmers were relieved to see the floodwaters finally ＿＿＿.`, o: [`exceed`, `proceed`, `succeed`, `recede`], a: 3, e: `recede（水）退去，re- 有「往回」之意，農夫「鬆一口氣」說明洪水退了。exceed 超過（需受詞）、proceed 繼續進行、succeed 成功，都不符合「洪水」作主詞的語境。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-03', d: 1, q: `The museum guide ＿＿＿ visitors not to touch the paintings, since the oil from human skin can damage them.`, o: [`reminds`, `admits`, `prevents`, `refuses`], a: 0, e: `remind sb. not to V 提醒某人不要做某事。prevent 的句型是 prevent sb. from V-ing，不接 not to；admit 承認、refuse 拒絕都不能接「人 + not to V」。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-04', d: 1, q: `The new medicine proved highly ＿＿＿ in reducing fever, but it caused serious side effects in some patients.`, o: [`expensive`, `extensive`, `effective`, `excessive`], a: 2, e: `be effective in V-ing 在…方面有效；but 後面轉向缺點（副作用），所以前面是優點。extensive 廣泛的、excessive 過度的、expensive 昂貴的，都不能搭配 in reducing fever。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-05', d: 1, q: `To ＿＿＿ the heavy traffic downtown, the city government plans to open a new subway line next year.`, o: [`conceal`, `reject`, `relieve`, `relate`], a: 2, e: `relieve 表示減輕、緩解，地鐵新增運輸容量可紓解市區交通，符合句子的目的。conceal 是隱藏、reject 是拒絕、relate 是使有關聯；這些動詞都無法表達蓋地鐵以減少塞車的意思。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-06', d: 2, q: `Kevin's handwriting was so ＿＿＿ that the teacher could barely read a single sentence of his essay.`, o: [`illegible`, `legible`, `fragile`, `vivid`], a: 0, e: `illegible 表示字跡難以辨認，正好解釋老師幾乎讀不出任何一句。legible 是清晰可辨的，與後面的 barely read 直接相反；fragile 是脆弱的、vivid 是生動的，也都沒有「字跡難認」的意思。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-07', d: 2, q: `The documentary ＿＿＿ the daily lives of fishermen on a small island, showing both their hardships and their joys.`, o: [`deprives`, `detects`, `deposits`, `depicts`], a: 3, e: `depict 描繪、描寫，紀錄片「呈現」漁民生活最合理，後面 showing… 也呼應。deprive 剝奪（deprive A of B）、detect 偵測、deposit 存放，皆字形相近的干擾。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-08', d: 2, q: `Although she was ＿＿＿ to speak in front of the whole school, Amy finally agreed to give the graduation speech.`, o: [`eager`, `reluctant`, `confident`, `grateful`], a: 1, e: `Although 表轉折：前面「不情願」，後面「終究答應」。reluctant 不情願的正好相反於 agreed。eager 渴望的、confident 有自信的都與 agreed 同方向，不需要 although。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-09', d: 2, q: `The airline offered free hotel rooms to ＿＿＿ passengers for the twelve-hour delay.`, o: [`compensate`, `compete`, `complain`, `congratulate`], a: 0, e: `compensate A for B 表示因 B 補償 A；航空公司提供免費住宿，是對十二小時誤點的補償。compete 是競爭、complain 是抱怨，不能在此直接接 passengers；congratulate 是祝賀，誤點並非值得祝賀的成就或喜事。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-10', d: 2, q: `With the birth rate falling for decades, many experts believe that a shrinking workforce is ＿＿＿; the only question is how fast it will happen.`, o: [`invisible`, `incredible`, `inevitable`, `innocent`], a: 2, e: `分號後「唯一的問題是會多快發生」表示「一定會發生」，即 inevitable 不可避免的。invisible 看不見的、incredible 難以置信的、innocent 無辜的都與後句定義不符。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-11', d: 1, q: `Thanks to the warm climate and ＿＿＿ rainfall, the island produces fruit all year round.`, o: [`scarce`, `abundant`, `ancient`, `destructive`], a: 1, e: `Thanks to 引出有利於全年產果的原因，因此雨量應是 abundant（充足的）。scarce 是稀少的，不能表達雨量充足；ancient 是古老的，與產果條件無關；destructive 是具破壞性的，與此處有利於全年產果的條件相反。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-12', d: 1, q: `Because of the approaching typhoon, the school decided to ＿＿＿ the sports day until next Friday.`, o: [`postpone`, `process`, `propose`, `protect`], a: 0, e: `postpone…until… 延期到某時，until next Friday 是關鍵線索。process 處理、propose 提議、protect 保護，都不能搭配 until 表延後。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-13', d: 3, q: `The restorer was so ＿＿＿ that she spent three days cleaning a single square inch of the old painting.`, o: [`reckless`, `spontaneous`, `indifferent`, `meticulous`], a: 3, e: `meticulous 一絲不苟的；花三天清一平方英寸，就是極度仔細。reckless 魯莽的、spontaneous 隨性的、indifferent 漠不關心的，都與「極度耐心」相反。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-14', d: 3, q: `Spreading rumors online can seriously ＿＿＿ public trust in the health system, especially during a pandemic.`, o: [`undermine`, `underline`, `undertake`, `undergo`], a: 0, e: `undermine 逐漸削弱、破壞（信任、權威、健康），謠言會「破壞」大眾信任。underline 畫底線／強調、undertake 承擔（任務）、undergo 經歷（手術、改變），都不能以 trust 為受詞表「破壞」。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-15', d: 2, q: `To build a healthy habit, being ＿＿＿ matters more than being intense; jogging twenty minutes a day beats running once a month for two hours.`, o: [`convenient`, `conscious`, `consistent`, `considerate`], a: 2, e: `分號後的例子「每天二十分鐘勝過每月一次兩小時」說明的是「持續一致」= consistent。convenient 方便的、conscious 有意識的、considerate 體貼的，都不是例子要表達的重點。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-16', d: 2, q: `Young children and the elderly are especially ＿＿＿ to heatstroke during summer heat waves.`, o: [`accessible`, `vulnerable`, `valuable`, `visible`], a: 1, e: `be vulnerable to 表示容易受到傷害，符合幼兒與長者在熱浪中較易中暑的語境。accessible 是可接近或可使用的、valuable 是有價值的、visible 是看得見的，都不能表達「容易受到中暑危害」。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-17', d: 3, q: `The contract's wording was so ＿＿＿ that both companies claimed it supported their own interpretation.`, o: [`unanimous`, `edible`, `nocturnal`, `ambiguous`], a: 3, e: `ambiguous（有歧義的）表示可有不只一種解讀，用來形容合約措辭，最符合雙方都宣稱措辭支持自己解讀的語境。unanimous 是全體一致的，通常形容群體意見或共同決議；edible 是可食用的；nocturnal 是夜行的、夜間活動的，三者都不符合此處合約措辭的特性。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-18', d: 1, q: `The fire was not an accident; investigators found evidence that someone had started it ＿＿＿.`, o: [`accidentally`, `deliberately`, `gradually`, `temporarily`], a: 1, e: `前句「不是意外」，後句說明有人「故意」放火 = deliberately。accidentally 與前句矛盾；gradually 逐漸地、temporarily 暫時地都與「意外與否」無關。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-19', d: 3, q: `Listening to soft music before an exam can help ＿＿＿ anxiety and improve concentration.`, o: [`aggravate`, `accumulate`, `alleviate`, `anticipate`], a: 2, e: `alleviate 減輕（痛苦、焦慮），與 improve concentration 同為正面效果。aggravate 使惡化正好相反；accumulate 累積、anticipate 預期，皆不合「help ___ anxiety」的正面語意。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-20', d: 2, q: `Once a quiet fishing village, the town has become ＿＿＿ since the port was expanded thirty years ago.`, o: [`prosperous`, `desperate`, `ordinary`, `isolated`], a: 0, e: `Once a quiet fishing village（曾是寧靜漁村）與港口擴建形成對比，表示城鎮變「繁榮」= prosperous。desperate 絕望的、isolated 孤立的與港口擴建的結果相反；ordinary 普通的無法形成對比。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-21', d: 3, q: `After working in finance for ten years, Leo is ＿＿＿ a career change; he has been taking cooking classes on weekends to see if he enjoys it.`, o: [`contradicting`, `confiscating`, `compensating`, `contemplating`], a: 3, e: `contemplate 仔細考慮（尚未決定），後句「上烹飪課看看自己喜不喜歡」正是在考慮轉行。contradict 反駁、confiscate 沒收、compensate 補償，均不合語意。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-22', d: 2, q: `Turn off your phone notifications; constant messages will only ＿＿＿ you from your studies.`, o: [`attract`, `extract`, `distract`, `contract`], a: 2, e: `distract A from B 使 A 從 B 分心。attract 吸引（attract A to B）、extract 萃取、contract 收縮／簽約，皆是同字根 -tract 的干擾項，與 from your studies 搭配不合理。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-23', d: 3, q: `The pandemic caused an ＿＿＿ drop in international travel; never before had so many airports been almost empty at the same time.`, o: [`unanimous`, `unprecedented`, `unconditional`, `unintentional`], a: 1, e: `分號後 never before（以前從未有過）就是 unprecedented 史無前例的定義。unanimous 全體一致的、unconditional 無條件的、unintentional 非故意的，都與「前所未有」無關。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-24', d: 1, q: `Many students do not get ＿＿＿ sleep during exam season, which actually lowers their test performance.`, o: [`adequate`, `accurate`, `adjacent`, `abstract`], a: 0, e: `adequate 足夠的，「睡眠不足反而降低表現」合理。accurate 準確的、adjacent 鄰近的、abstract 抽象的，皆不能修飾 sleep 表「量」。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-25', d: 3, q: `The ＿＿＿ speaker held the audience's attention for an hour with her clear arguments and vivid stories.`, o: [`hesitant`, `monotonous`, `obscure`, `eloquent`], a: 3, e: `eloquent 口才流利、有說服力的，與「論點清楚、故事生動、抓住聽眾一小時」一致。hesitant 猶豫的、monotonous 單調的、obscure 晦澀的，都會讓聽眾分心。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-26', d: 2, q: `Using bamboo, which grows back quickly after being cut, is considered a more ＿＿＿ choice than using hardwood from old forests.`, o: [`temporary`, `sustainable`, `fragile`, `nonrenewable`], a: 1, e: `竹子砍下後很快再生，這個線索支持 sustainable（可持續利用的）。temporary 是暫時的、fragile 是脆弱的，都與再生能力無直接關係；nonrenewable 是不可再生的，更與題目所述的快速再生相反。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-27', d: 3, q: `Scientists remained ＿＿＿ of the company's claim that its drink could cure cancer, since no clinical data had been published.`, o: [`optimistic`, `enthusiastic`, `skeptical`, `supportive`], a: 2, e: `be skeptical of 對…存疑；理由是「沒有公布任何臨床數據」。optimistic、enthusiastic、supportive 都是正面態度，與「缺乏證據」的理由矛盾。` },
      { id: 'en-vocab-28', d: 1, q: `Instead of throwing away old clothes, you can ＿＿＿ them to charities that give them to people in need.`, o: [`decorate`, `dominate`, `demonstrate`, `donate`], a: 3, e: `donate A to B 把 A 捐給 B，charities（慈善機構）是線索。decorate 裝飾、dominate 支配、demonstrate 示範，都不搭配 to charities。` }
    ]
  },

  /* ───────────────────────── 2. 片語與搭配詞 ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-phrase',
    name: '片語與搭配詞',
    notes: [
      { t: '可分離片語動詞', b: '受詞是<b>代名詞</b>時必須放中間：look <b>it</b> up、turn <b>it</b> off、put <b>them</b> away；寫成 look up it 是錯的。受詞是名詞時兩種位置都可以：look up the word = look the word up。' },
      { t: 'take 家族', b: 'take effect 生效（法規、藥）／take place 發生、舉行（主詞是事件）／take part in 參加／take after 長得像（長輩）／take over 接管／take up 開始從事（嗜好）。' },
      { t: '三字片語必背', b: 'put up with 忍受、make up for 彌補、come up with 想出、catch up with 趕上、look up to 尊敬、look down on 輕視、run out of 用完、get rid of 擺脫。' },
      { t: '因果方向別搞反', b: 'A result<b>s in</b> B = A lead<b>s to</b> B（A 導致 B）；B result<b>s from</b> A（B 起因於 A）。attribute B <b>to</b> A：把 B 歸因於 A。' },
      { t: '動詞＋名詞搭配', b: 'play a role（扮演角色）、make a decision、pay attention to、fill out a form、call off a meeting、convert A into B。' }
    ],
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-phrase-01', d: 1, q: `Please ＿＿＿ the form and hand it to the nurse at the front desk.`, o: [`fill out`, `give up`, `put off`, `look after`], a: 0, e: `fill out a form 填寫表格，填完再交給護理師。give up 放棄、put off 延後、look after 照顧，都不是「表格」的搭配。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-02', d: 1, q: `The meeting was ＿＿＿ because the manager suddenly got sick.`, o: [`set up`, `carried on`, `called off`, `turned up`], a: 2, e: `call off 取消，經理突然生病所以會議取消。set up 安排、carried on 繼續進行都與原因矛盾；turn up 出現，被動用法不合理。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-03', d: 1, q: `I can't ＿＿＿ his rude behavior any longer; I'm going to talk to him today.`, o: [`come up with`, `put up with`, `make up for`, `catch up with`], a: 1, e: `put up with 忍受，can't…any longer「再也忍不下去」。come up with 想出、make up for 彌補、catch up with 趕上，都不能與 rude behavior 搭配表達情緒。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-04', d: 2, q: `Taiwan's night markets ＿＿＿ a major role in attracting international tourists.`, o: [`make`, `take`, `do`, `play`], a: 3, e: `固定搭配 play a(n) (important/major) role in…「在…扮演重要角色」。make、take、do 都不與 role 搭配，是中式英文常見錯誤。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-05', d: 2, q: `Leo stayed up late to ＿＿＿ the time he had wasted playing video games.`, o: [`look up to`, `run out of`, `make up for`, `get along with`], a: 2, e: `make up for 彌補（損失、浪費的時間）。look up to 尊敬、run out of 用完、get along with 與…相處，都不合「熬夜補回浪費的時間」。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-06', d: 2, q: `The detective promised to ＿＿＿ the cause of the mysterious fire.`, o: [`look forward to`, `look into`, `look down on`, `look up to`], a: 1, e: `look into 調查 = investigate。look forward to 期待、look down on 輕視、look up to 尊敬，皆是 look 家族的干擾，與「起火原因」不搭。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-07', d: 2, q: `We have almost ＿＿＿ water; we must find a stream before dark.`, o: [`taken over`, `made up for`, `gotten rid of`, `run out of`], a: 3, e: `run out of 用完（run 的過去分詞仍是 run），水快用完所以要找溪流。get rid of 擺脫（不想要的東西）、take over 接管、make up for 彌補，皆不合語意。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-08', d: 3, q: `The new policy will ＿＿＿ next month, so all employees must learn the new rules by then.`, o: [`take effect`, `take place`, `take part`, `take turns`], a: 0, e: `take effect（法規、政策、藥物）生效。take place 指「事件、活動舉行」，政策不是活動，是常見誤用；take part（in）參加、take turns 輪流皆不合。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-09', d: 2, q: `She ＿＿＿ her grandmother in many ways — they both love gardening and telling jokes.`, o: [`takes over`, `takes after`, `takes off`, `takes up`], a: 1, e: `take after（外貌、個性）像長輩，破折號後說明兩人共同的喜好。take over 接管、take off 起飛／脫下、take up 開始從事，都無「相像」之意。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-10', d: 3, q: `The old factory has been ＿＿＿ into an art center where young designers display their works.`, o: [`conveyed`, `convinced`, `converted`, `consisted`], a: 2, e: `convert A into B 把 A 改造成 B，被動為 A be converted into B。convey 傳達、convince 說服；consist（of）由…組成是不及物動詞，不能用被動。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-11', d: 3, q: `Despite the heavy rain, the marathon ＿＿＿ as planned.`, o: [`broke down`, `went ahead`, `gave in`, `fell apart`], a: 1, e: `go ahead（活動）照常進行，Despite 表示「雖然下雨仍照計畫」。break down 故障／崩潰、give in 屈服、fall apart 瓦解，與 as planned 互相矛盾。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-12', d: 2, q: `If you don't understand a word, you can ＿＿＿ in the dictionary.`, o: [`look it up`, `look up it`, `look up for it`, `look it down`], a: 0, e: `look up（查詢）是可分離片語，受詞為代名詞 it 時必須放中間：look it up。look up it 語序錯誤；look up for、look down 都不是「查字典」。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-13', d: 1, q: `The teacher asked us to ＿＿＿ our phones during the exam.`, o: [`put up`, `put out`, `put off`, `put away`], a: 3, e: `put away 收起來。put up 張貼／搭建、put out 熄滅（火）、put off 延後，都不是考試時對手機的指示。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-14', d: 3, q: `Her success was not a matter of luck; it ＿＿＿ years of hard work.`, o: [`resulted in`, `contributed to`, `resulted from`, `ran out of`], a: 2, e: `it 指成功，是結果；years of hard work 是原因，所以要用 resulted from（起因於）。resulted in 表示導致，contributed to 表示促成，都把成功說成努力的原因，顛倒此處因果；ran out of 表示用完或耗盡，不能把成功說成耗盡多年努力的主體。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-15', d: 1, q: `Thank you for ＿＿＿ my cat while I was on vacation.`, o: [`taking care of`, `taking part in`, `taking up`, `taking off`], a: 0, e: `take care of 照顧，度假時請人照顧貓。take part in 參加、take up 開始從事、take off 起飛／脫下，對象都不會是「貓」。` },
      { id: 'en-phrase-16', d: 3, q: `The scientist ＿＿＿ her success to the support of her team.`, o: [`contributed`, `distributed`, `devoted`, `attributed`], a: 3, e: `attribute A to B 把 A 歸功於 B。contribute A to B 是「貢獻 A 給 B」，她不是把成功捐給團隊；distribute 分配、devote A to B 把 A 奉獻於 B，語意都不對。` }
    ]
  },

  /* ───────────────────────── 3. 文法核心 I ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-gram1',
    name: '文法 I：時態·假設·倒裝·分詞',
    notes: [
      { t: '假設語氣三公式', b: '與現在相反：If S + 過去式（be 一律 were），S + would/could + 原形。<br>與過去相反：If S + had p.p.，S + would/could have p.p.。<br>混合（過去條件→現在結果）：If S + had p.p.，S + would + 原形 + now。' },
      { t: '省略 if 的倒裝', b: 'If I were → <b>Were</b> I…；If I had known → <b>Had</b> I known…；If you should need → <b>Should</b> you need…。要不是：If it had not been for = Had it not been for = Without / But for。' },
      { t: '倒裝句觸發詞', b: '否定副詞放句首要倒裝（助動詞/be 動詞提前）：Never, Seldom, Rarely, Little, Hardly…when, No sooner…than, Not until…, Only + 副詞（片語/子句）。另有 So + adj. + be + S + that…。' },
      { t: '分詞構句', b: '兩子句主詞相同才能省略主詞：主動用 V-ing、被動用 p.p.、先發生的動作用 Having p.p.。主詞不同時要保留主詞：<b>The weather being</b> fine, we went out.（獨立分詞構句）' },
      { t: '時態關鍵字', b: 'by the time + 過去式 → 主句過去完成；since + 過去時間點 → 現在完成；This time next week → 未來進行式。' },
      { t: '建議/要求動詞與 high time', b: 'suggest / insist / demand / require that S + (should) + <b>原形</b>。It is (high) time that S + <b>過去式</b>（表示早該做了）。' }
    ],
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-gram1-01', d: 1, q: `By the time we got to the theater, the movie ＿＿＿.`, o: [`already started`, `has already started`, `had already started`, `would already start`], a: 2, e: `By the time + 過去式（got），主要子句的動作更早完成，用過去完成式 had p.p.。現在完成式不能與過去時間的參照點搭配；過去簡單式無法表現「先後」。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-02', d: 1, q: `I ＿＿＿ in Tainan since I was ten, so I know every good food stand in town.`, o: [`lived`, `have lived`, `had lived`, `am living`], a: 1, e: `since + 過去時間點，表示從過去持續到現在，用現在完成式 have lived。lived 只表過去；had lived 需要一個過去參照點；am living 不能與 since 連用。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-03', d: 1, q: `If I ＿＿＿ you, I would apologize to her right away.`, o: [`am`, `had been`, `will be`, `were`], a: 3, e: `與現在事實相反的假設（我不可能是你），if 子句用過去式，be 動詞一律用 were。had been 是與過去相反；am、will be 是直說法，不搭配 would。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-04', d: 2, q: `If Tom had taken the doctor's advice last year, he ＿＿＿ so sick now.`, o: [`wouldn't be`, `wouldn't have been`, `won't be`, `hadn't been`], a: 0, e: `混合假設：條件是去年（與過去相反 had taken），結果是 now（與現在相反），所以主句用 would + 原形。wouldn't have been 會變成「當時不會生病」，與 now 衝突。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-05', d: 2, q: `＿＿＿ I known about the test, I would have studied harder.`, o: [`If`, `Should`, `Were`, `Had`], a: 3, e: `原句是 If I had known…，省略 if 後把 had 移到句首倒裝：Had I known…。If I known 缺少 had；Should / Were 倒裝分別對應未來可能與現在假設，接不上 known。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-06', d: 2, q: `Never ＿＿＿ such a beautiful sunset as the one we saw in Kenting.`, o: [`I have seen`, `have I seen`, `I saw`, `saw I`], a: 1, e: `否定副詞 Never 置於句首須倒裝：Never + 助動詞 + S + V。have I seen 正確。I have seen 沒倒裝；saw I 是把一般動詞直接提前，英文不行（要用 did I see）。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-07', d: 2, q: `Only after the teacher explained it again ＿＿＿ the main idea of the poem.`, o: [`I understood`, `understood I`, `did I understand`, `I did understand`], a: 2, e: `Only + 副詞子句放句首，主要子句要倒裝；一般動詞的過去式需借助 did：did I understand。注意倒裝的是「主要子句」，after 子句本身不倒裝。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-08', d: 2, q: `＿＿＿ from the top of Taipei 101, the city looks like a field of tiny lights.`, o: [`Seen`, `Seeing`, `To see`, `Having seen`], a: 0, e: `主要子句主詞是 the city，城市是「被看到」的，被動用過去分詞 Seen。Seeing / Having seen 表示城市主動去看，主詞不符；To see 不定詞在此成為懸垂結構。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-09', d: 2, q: `＿＿＿ his homework, Ben went out to play basketball.`, o: [`Finished`, `To finish`, `Being finished`, `Having finished`], a: 3, e: `先寫完功課、再出去打球，先發生且為主動，用完成式分詞 Having finished。Finished / Being finished 是被動，Ben 不是被完成的；To finish 表目的，與「出去打球」邏輯不符。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-10', d: 3, q: `It is high time that we ＿＿＿ action to protect the coral reefs.`, o: [`take`, `will take`, `took`, `have taken`], a: 2, e: `It is (high) time that S + 過去式，表「早該做了（但還沒做）」，是一種假設語氣，所以用 took。take / will take / have taken 都是直說法時態。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-11', d: 3, q: `The doctor suggested that my father ＿＿＿ smoking immediately.`, o: [`stops`, `stop`, `stopped`, `would stop`], a: 1, e: `suggest（建議）that S + (should) + 原形動詞，主詞是第三人稱也不加 s，所以是 stop。stops、stopped、would stop 都忽略了建議類動詞後的原形規則（英式口語偶見 stopped 等直說法，但正式文法與學測一律以原形為準）。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-12', d: 3, q: `Without your help, I ＿＿＿ the project on time last week.`, o: [`won't be able to finish`, `hadn't finished`, `couldn't have finish`, `couldn't have finished`], a: 3, e: `Without your help 在此表達對過去情況的假設，last week 指向過去，主句用 couldn't have finished，表示若無協助就無法完成。won't be able to finish 指未來，與 last week 不合；hadn't finished 缺少假設主句所需的情態形式；couldn't have finish 則錯在 have 後須接過去分詞 finished。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-13', d: 2, q: `The weather ＿＿＿ fine, we decided to go hiking in Yangmingshan.`, o: [`was`, `being`, `is`, `been`], a: 1, e: `兩個子句之間只有逗號、沒有連接詞，前半必須改成分詞構句；主詞（the weather / we）不同，要保留主詞成為獨立分詞構句 The weather being fine。用 was 會變成兩個句子直接以逗號相連（逗號錯接）。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-14', d: 3, q: `So loud ＿＿＿ that the neighbors called the police.`, o: [`was the music`, `the music was`, `did the music`, `the music did`], a: 0, e: `So + adj. 放句首要倒裝：So loud was the music that…（原句 The music was so loud that…）。be 動詞直接提前，不需要 did；the music was 沒有倒裝。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-15', d: 2, q: `This time next week, we ＿＿＿ on a beach in Okinawa.`, o: [`will be lying`, `have lain`, `were lying`, `had lain`], a: 0, e: `This time next week（下週此時）表示未來某時間點正在進行的動作，用未來進行式 will be V-ing。lie（躺）的變化是 lie-lay-lain-lying，其他選項時態都不對應未來。` },
      { id: 'en-gram1-16', d: 3, q: `下列哪一句文法正確？`, o: [`Hardly did I sit down when the phone rang.`, `Hardly I had sat down when the phone rang.`, `Hardly had I sat down when the phone rang.`, `Hardly had I sat down than the phone rang.`], a: 2, e: `「一…就…」句型：Hardly / Scarcely + had + S + p.p. + when + S + 過去式。Hardly 放句首要倒裝（B 沒倒裝）；搭配的是 when，than 是與 No sooner 搭配（D 錯）；前半應為過去完成式（A 錯）。` }
    ]
  },

  /* ───────────────────────── 4. 文法核心 II ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-gram2',
    name: '文法 II：子句·比較·轉折語',
    notes: [
      { t: '關係詞怎麼選', b: '看子句「缺什麼」：缺主詞或受詞 → 關係代名詞（who / which / that）；缺所有格 → whose；子句完整、缺地點/時間/原因 → 關係副詞 where / when / why（= 介系詞 + which）。<br>陷阱：The reason <b>which</b> he gave… 因為 gave 缺受詞，不能用 why。' },
      { t: '逗號後的 which', b: '非限定用法（逗號後）不能用 that；, which 可以指前面<b>整句話</b>：He passed the test, which surprised everyone.' },
      { t: '名詞子句', b: 'that 引導完整子句；what 引導缺主詞/受詞的子句（What surprised me was…）。「是否」當主詞要用 Whether，不能用 If。' },
      { t: '比較句型', b: 'The 比較級 + S + V, the 比較級 + S + V（越…越…）。倍數：twice / three times + as…as。比較對象要一致：The population of A is larger than <b>that</b> of B。最高級改寫：No other + 單數名詞 + is as…as / 比較級 than…；A is higher than any <b>other</b> + 單數名詞。' },
      { t: '轉折語分類表', b: '<b>轉折</b> However, Nevertheless, Still, Yet｜<b>因果</b> Therefore, Thus, As a result, Consequently｜<b>補充</b> Moreover, Besides, In addition｜<b>舉例</b> For example, For instance｜<b>對比</b> In contrast, On the other hand, while, whereas｜<b>否則</b> Otherwise｜<b>換句話說</b> In other words。' },
      { t: '連接詞 vs. 介系詞', b: 'although / though / even though + 子句；despite / in spite of + 名詞或 V-ing。because + 子句；because of + 名詞。' }
    ],
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-gram2-01', d: 1, q: `The girl ＿＿＿ is standing by the window is my cousin.`, o: [`which`, `who`, `whose`, `whom`], a: 1, e: `先行詞 the girl 是人，關係子句缺主詞（___ is standing），用主格 who。which 用於物；whom 是受格；whose 是所有格，後面要接名詞。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-02', d: 2, q: `This is the small town ＿＿＿ my grandparents met sixty years ago.`, o: [`which`, `that`, `what`, `where`], a: 3, e: `my grandparents met sixty years ago 是完整子句（met 在此為不及物），缺的是地點，用關係副詞 where（= in which）。which / that 需要子句缺主詞或受詞；what 不能接先行詞。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-03', d: 2, q: `I visited the old house, ＿＿＿ roof had been damaged by the typhoon.`, o: [`whose`, `which`, `that`, `its`], a: 0, e: `the house's roof → whose roof，關係代名詞所有格。which 後面不能直接接 roof 當主詞；that 不能放逗號後；its 會讓兩個子句只用逗號連接，成為逗號錯接。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-04', d: 2, q: `＿＿＿ she will accept the job offer is still unknown.`, o: [`If`, `Which`, `Whether`, `What`], a: 2, e: `「她是否會接受」當主詞，用 Whether 引導名詞子句；If 引導「是否」時不能放句首當主詞。子句 she will accept the job offer 結構完整，所以 What / Which 不合。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-05', d: 2, q: `＿＿＿ surprised everyone was that the shy boy won the singing contest.`, o: [`That`, `It`, `Which`, `What`], a: 3, e: `___ surprised everyone 缺主詞，需要「先行詞 + 關係代名詞」合一的 What（= the thing that）。That 引導的名詞子句必須完整；It 會造成一句兩個動詞（surprised、was）。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-06', d: 1, q: `The more you practice, ＿＿＿ you will become.`, o: [`more confident`, `the most confident`, `the more confident`, `the confident more`], a: 2, e: `「越…越…」句型：The 比較級…, the 比較級…，前後都要有 the 加比較級。the most 是最高級；more confident 少了 the。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-07', d: 2, q: `The population of Taipei is larger than ＿＿＿ of Tainan.`, o: [`that`, `those`, `it`, `one`], a: 0, e: `比較的是「台北的人口」與「台南的人口」，要用 that 代替前面的單數名詞 the population。those 代替複數名詞；it 只能指同一個東西（不能被 of 片語修飾）；one 是代替可數名詞的「同類另一個」，比較句型中「the + 名詞 + of…」固定用 that / those 代替。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-08', d: 2, q: `This smartphone is ＿＿＿ the one I bought last year.`, o: [`twice expensive as`, `twice as expensive as`, `as twice expensive as`, `twice as expensive than`], a: 1, e: `倍數比較：倍數 + as + 原級 + as。倍數要放在第一個 as 之前；as…as 結構不能和 than 混用。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-09', d: 1, q: `The plan sounds great on paper. ＿＿＿, it would cost far more money than we have.`, o: [`Therefore`, `Moreover`, `However`, `For example`], a: 2, e: `前句「計畫聽起來很棒」，後句「但花費遠超預算」，語意轉折用 However。Therefore 表因果、Moreover 表補充、For example 表舉例，都不符合前後相反的關係。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-10', d: 1, q: `Lisa studied very hard; ＿＿＿, she passed the exam with the highest score in her class.`, o: [`as a result`, `in contrast`, `nevertheless`, `otherwise`], a: 0, e: `努力讀書 → 考最高分，是因果關係，用 as a result。in contrast 表對比、nevertheless 表轉折（儘管如此）、otherwise 表「否則」，都不合。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-11', d: 3, q: `Eat more vegetables; ＿＿＿, you may have trouble with digestion.`, o: [`therefore`, `likewise`, `besides`, `otherwise`], a: 3, e: `前句是建議，後句是「不這樣做的後果」，用 otherwise（否則）。therefore 會變成「吃蔬菜所以消化不良」，邏輯相反；likewise 同樣地、besides 此外都不合。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-12', d: 1, q: `＿＿＿ it was raining heavily, the soccer game continued.`, o: [`Despite`, `Although`, `Because`, `Unless`], a: 1, e: `前後語意相反（下大雨 vs. 比賽繼續），且後接完整子句，用連接詞 Although。Despite 是介系詞，只能接名詞或 V-ing（Despite the heavy rain）；Because、Unless 邏輯不符。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-13', d: 3, q: `Some students prefer studying in the library, ＿＿＿ others find it easier to focus at home.`, o: [`while`, `because`, `unless`, `so that`], a: 0, e: `while 在此表「對比」（= whereas），Some… while others… 是常見句型。because 表原因、unless 表條件、so that 表目的，都無法連接兩種對照的偏好。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-14', d: 3, q: `He is the only one of the students who ＿＿＿ passed the exam.`, o: [`have`, `has`, `having`, `to have`], a: 1, e: `the only one of + 複數名詞 + who…，關係子句的先行詞是 the only one（單數），動詞用單數 has。若沒有 the only，one of the students who… 的先行詞才是複數 students。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-15', d: 3, q: `下列哪一句文法與語意都正確？`, o: [`Yushan is higher than any mountain in Taiwan.`, `Yushan is higher than any other mountains in Taiwan.`, `No other mountain in Taiwan is as high as Yushan.`, `Yushan is the higher mountain in Taiwan.`], a: 2, e: `「No other + 單數名詞 + as…as」是最高級的改寫，正確。A 少了 other，等於說玉山比自己還高；B 的 any other 後要接單數 mountain；D 比較級前加 the 只用於「兩者之中」，台灣山不只兩座。` },
      { id: 'en-gram2-16', d: 3, q: `The reason ＿＿＿ he gave for being late was hard to believe.`, o: [`why`, `how`, `where`, `which`], a: 3, e: `he gave ___ 缺受詞（give 的受詞就是 the reason），所以要用關係代名詞 which（或 that）。看到 reason 就選 why 是陷阱：只有子句完整時（the reason why he was late）才用 why。` }
    ]
  },

  /* ───────────────────────── 5. 綜合測驗 ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-cloze',
    name: '綜合測驗',
    notes: [
      { t: '四大考點', b: '綜合測驗每格通常只考一件事：①字彙語意 ②文法結構（時態、詞性、分詞）③轉折語 ④片語搭配。先判斷「這格在考哪一種」，再選答案。' },
      { t: '先讀全文再作答', b: '空格的線索常在<b>下一句</b>。例如 nervous 的線索是後面的 fearing…，However 的線索是後面 worries disappeared。' },
      { t: '時態跟著全文走', b: '記敘文多用過去式，說明文多用現在式；並列動詞（sat … and guided）時態要一致。' },
      { t: '句尾分詞表結果', b: '…, V-ing… 常用來補充前一動作帶來的結果：invite children to visit, <b>giving</b> them a rare chance…；逗號後接 which 則可指前面整件事。' }
    ],
    passages: {
      c1: `In recent years, a growing number of city dwellers have started keeping bees on rooftops. At first, many neighbors were ＿(1)＿ about the idea, fearing that the insects would sting their children. ＿(2)＿, most of these worries disappeared once people learned that honeybees rarely attack unless they feel threatened.<br><br>Urban beekeepers point out that cities can actually be good homes for bees. Parks, street trees, and balcony gardens ＿(3)＿ a wide variety of flowers, and city bees are often exposed to fewer pesticides than those living near large farms. Besides producing honey, the bees help pollinate local plants, ＿(4)＿ benefits the whole neighborhood. Some rooftop projects even invite schoolchildren to visit, ＿(5)＿ them a rare chance to see how a hive works up close.`,
      c2: `When my grandmother turned seventy-five, she announced that she wanted to learn how to use a smartphone. My family was surprised, ＿(1)＿ she had always said that modern technology was too complicated for her. Her reason, ＿(2)＿, was simple: she wanted to see photos of her great-grandson, who lives in Canada.<br><br>Every Sunday afternoon, I sat beside her and ＿(3)＿ her through the basic steps — unlocking the screen, opening apps, and making video calls. Progress was slow. She often forgot what she had learned the week before, and sometimes she became so ＿(4)＿ that she wanted to throw the phone away. But she never gave up. Three months later, she made her first video call all by herself. When her great-grandson's face appeared on the screen, she ＿(5)＿ into tears of joy.`,
      c3: `Many teenagers today spend more than seven hours a day looking at screens. To help young people ＿(1)＿ this habit, some summer camps have begun to ban phones completely. Campers must hand in their devices on the first day, and they ＿(2)＿ them back until the camp ends a week later.<br><br>The first two days are usually the hardest. Many campers say they feel anxious and keep reaching for phones that are no longer in their pockets. ＿(3)＿ the third day, however, most of them begin to relax. Without notifications ＿(4)＿ their attention, they talk more with one another, notice the stars at night, and sleep better.<br><br>Of course, a week without phones will not change a person's habits forever. ＿(5)＿, many campers say the experience made them realize how much time they had been giving away to their screens.`
    },
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-cloze-01', d: 1, pid: 'c1', q: `第 1 格最適合填入？`, o: [`enthusiastic`, `grateful`, `nervous`, `curious`], a: 2, e: `後面的分詞構句 fearing that the insects would sting their children（擔心蜜蜂螫小孩）說明鄰居的情緒是「緊張、擔心」，be nervous about。enthusiastic、grateful 是正面情緒，與 fearing 矛盾；curious 好奇沒有「害怕」之意。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-02', d: 1, pid: 'c1', q: `第 2 格最適合填入？`, o: [`However`, `Therefore`, `Similarly`, `For instance`], a: 0, e: `前面鄰居擔心，後面擔心消失了，語意轉折用 However。Therefore 表因果、Similarly 表類似、For instance 表舉例，都無法連接「擔心 → 不擔心」的轉變。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-03', d: 2, pid: 'c1', q: `第 3 格最適合填入？`, o: [`deprive`, `replace`, `consume`, `provide`], a: 3, e: `公園、行道樹與陽台花園「提供」多樣的花，正好說明城市為何適合蜜蜂，provide 正確。deprive 剝奪（需 of）、replace 取代、consume 消耗，都與「城市是好家園」的論點相反。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-04', d: 2, pid: 'c1', q: `第 4 格最適合填入？`, o: [`that`, `which`, `what`, `it`], a: 1, e: `逗號後要接非限定關係代名詞 which，指前面整件事（蜜蜂幫忙授粉），並當 benefits 的主詞。that 不能放在逗號後；what 不需先行詞；it 會造成兩個子句以逗號相連的錯誤。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-05', d: 2, pid: 'c1', q: `第 5 格最適合填入？`, o: [`gave`, `given`, `giving`, `gives`], a: 2, e: `句子已有主要動詞 invite，逗號後用分詞構句表「附帶結果」：giving them a rare chance（因此給了孩子難得機會）。gave / gives 會多出一個沒有連接詞的動詞；given 是被動，後面又接了受詞 them，不合。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-06', d: 1, pid: 'c2', q: `第 1 格最適合填入？`, o: [`although`, `unless`, `so`, `because`], a: 3, e: `家人很驚訝，「因為」奶奶一向說科技太複雜，because 說明驚訝的原因。although 讓前後變成讓步，邏輯不通；so 會把因果倒過來；unless 表條件不合。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-07', d: 2, pid: 'c2', q: `第 2 格最適合填入？`, o: [`therefore`, `however`, `for example`, `in addition`], a: 1, e: `上一句說科技「太複雜」，這句說她的理由「很簡單」，形成對照，用插入語 however。therefore 表結果、for example 表舉例、in addition 表補充，皆無轉折之意。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-08', d: 2, pid: 'c2', q: `第 3 格最適合填入？`, o: [`guided`, `guiding`, `to guide`, `guide`], a: 0, e: `and 連接兩個對等的動詞：I sat … and guided…，時態要和 sat 一致用過去式。guide 是現在式，與全文過去式不一致；guiding、to guide 放在 and 後無法與 sat 對等。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-09', d: 2, pid: 'c2', q: `第 4 格最適合填入？`, o: [`frustrating`, `delighted`, `frustrated`, `relaxed`], a: 2, e: `奶奶「感到」挫折，人的感受用過去分詞 frustrated；frustrating 是「令人挫折的」，形容事物。delighted、relaxed 是正面情緒，與「想把手機丟掉」矛盾。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-10', d: 1, pid: 'c2', q: `第 5 格最適合填入？`, o: [`came`, `burst`, `made`, `put`], a: 1, e: `burst into tears 突然大哭，是固定搭配（類似 burst into laughter 突然大笑）。came / made / put 都不和 into tears 搭配。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-11', d: 1, pid: 'c3', q: `第 1 格最適合填入？`, o: [`keep`, `form`, `break`, `follow`], a: 2, e: `營隊禁止手機，目的是幫青少年「戒掉」長時間看螢幕的習慣，break a habit。keep、form、follow 都是維持或養成習慣，與禁手機的目的相反。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-12', d: 2, pid: 'c3', q: `第 2 格最適合填入？`, o: [`didn't get`, `haven't got`, `hadn't got`, `won't get`], a: 3, e: `本段描述營隊的一般規定，用現在式（must hand in），「營隊結束前不會拿回」是未來動作，用 won't get…until…（直到…才）。didn't、hadn't 是過去時態，與規則說明不符；haven't got 表完成，與 until 未來時間點不合。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-13', d: 2, pid: 'c3', q: `第 3 格最適合填入？`, o: [`By`, `Since`, `Until`, `For`], a: 0, e: `By the third day「到了第三天」，表示到這時候已經開始放鬆。Since 需搭配完成式；Until the third day 意思變成「直到第三天前都在放鬆」，與前文前兩天最難熬矛盾；For 後接一段時間，不接時間點。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-14', d: 3, pid: 'c3', q: `第 4 格最適合填入？`, o: [`competed for`, `competing for`, `compete for`, `competes for`], a: 1, e: `介系詞 without + 受詞 + V-ing / p.p. 的句型（with/without 獨立結構）。通知是主動「爭奪」注意力，用 competing for。competed for 是被動語意不合；compete / competes 是限定動詞，不能放在介系詞片語中。` },
      { id: 'en-cloze-15', d: 2, pid: 'c3', q: `第 5 格最適合填入？`, o: [`Therefore`, `In other words`, `Still`, `As a result`], a: 2, e: `前句承認「一週無手機不會永遠改變習慣」，後句卻說「許多人因此意識到花了多少時間」，是讓步後的轉折，用 Still（儘管如此）。Therefore / As a result 表因果，In other words 表換句話說，都不合。` }
    ]
  },

  /* ───────────────────────── 6. 篇章結構／文意選填 ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-struct',
    name: '篇章結構與文意選填',
    notes: [
      { t: '找連結線索', b: '候選句的<b>代名詞</b>（them, these, it）與<b>指示詞</b>（this, such）一定指向前一句提過的東西；<b>轉折詞</b>（However, For example, As a result）要和前後邏輯一致。' },
      { t: '解題順序', b: '先填線索最明確的空格，最後一格用刪去法。填完後把「前一句＋候選句＋後一句」連讀一次確認。' },
      { t: '誘答句長什麼樣', b: '主題相關但離題（講到別的動物、別的人）、與文意矛盾、或轉折詞方向錯誤（As a result 接不上因果）。' },
      { t: '文意選填技巧', b: '先判斷空格詞性（冠詞＋___＋名詞 → 形容詞；主詞＋___＋受詞 → 動詞並注意單複數），再看語意正負與上下文。' }
    ],
    passages: {
      s1: `Many cat owners have noticed their pets pressing their paws in and out against a soft blanket or even a person's lap, a behavior often called "kneading." ＿(1)＿ Newborn kittens knead their mother's belly while nursing, which helps the milk flow. Many cats simply carry this habit into adulthood. ＿(2)＿ For this reason, an adult cat that kneads on your lap is probably feeling safe and relaxed.<br><br>There may be another purpose as well. Cats have scent glands in the soft pads of their paws. ＿(3)＿ In other words, when your cat kneads your sweater, it may be quietly announcing that you belong to it.`,
      s2: `Do you find it hard to concentrate for long periods? The Pomodoro Technique, a simple time-management method, might help. ＿(1)＿ You work on a single task for 25 minutes, then take a five-minute break. After four rounds, you take a longer break of 15 to 30 minutes.<br><br>＿(2)＿ Knowing that a break is coming soon makes it easier to resist distractions such as phone messages. The method also helps people see how long tasks really take. ＿(3)＿ Over time, users learn to plan their days more realistically.`,
      s3: `Visitors to Taiwan are often amazed by its convenience stores. ＿(1)＿ But what really surprises foreigners is not their number but the range of services they offer. Customers can pay utility bills, pick up online orders, print documents, and even buy train tickets at the counter. ＿(2)＿ This is especially helpful for people who work late or start work early.<br><br>Many stores also provide seating areas, where students do homework and office workers eat lunch. ＿(3)＿ For many people in Taiwan, the convenience store is not just a shop; it is part of daily life, like a neighbor who never sleeps.`,
      s4: `Light pollution is a growing problem in modern cities. Bright streetlights and glowing billboards make it difficult to see the night sky; in some downtown areas, only a few of the ＿(1)＿ stars are visible. Artificial light also ＿(2)＿ wildlife. Baby sea turtles, for example, normally find the sea by heading toward the brighter horizon over the water, but city lights can lead them in the wrong direction. Fortunately, the problem is not ＿(3)＿. Using shielded lamps that point downward and turning off unnecessary lights can greatly reduce it.`
    },
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-struct-01', d: 2, pid: 's1', q: `第 1 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`By pressing them against an object, a cat leaves behind its own smell.`, `Scientists believe the behavior begins very early in life.`, `To them, kneading is linked to the comfort and warmth they felt as babies.`, `Dogs, on the other hand, rarely show this kind of behavior.`], a: 1, e: `下一句馬上談「剛出生的小貓」，所以此格要先點出「這行為從很小就開始」，(B) 正確。(D) 講狗，離題；(A) 的 them 指肉球，此時文章還沒提到。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-02', d: 2, pid: 's1', q: `第 2 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`An adult cat kneading on a person's lap feels threatened rather than relaxed.`, `Scientists believe the behavior begins very early in life.`, `Kneading reminds them of the comfort and warmth they felt as kittens.`, `Dogs, on the other hand, rarely show this kind of behavior.`], a: 2, e: `前句說貓把踩踏習慣帶到成年，後句用 For this reason 推論在腿上踩踏代表安心。中間須說明踩踏讓貓想起幼時的舒適溫暖，因此選 (C)；them 指 cats。(A) 把文中的安心放鬆反說成受威脅；(B) 只談起始年齡，未解釋安心；(D) 談狗，離開主題。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-03', d: 2, pid: 's1', q: `第 3 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`By pressing them against an object, a cat leaves behind its own smell.`, `Scientists believe the behavior begins very early in life.`, `To them, kneading is linked to the comfort and warmth they felt as babies.`, `Dogs, on the other hand, rarely show this kind of behavior.`], a: 0, e: `前句提到肉球有氣味腺，(A) 的 them 正好指 pads，並說明踩踏會留下氣味；後句 In other words 再換句話說「宣示你是牠的」。(D) 講狗與段落主旨無關，是誘答句。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-04', d: 1, pid: 's2', q: `第 1 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`The basic idea is surprisingly easy to follow.`, `For example, a student may discover that a report she expected to finish in one round actually takes four.`, `Many people prefer to study late at night when the house is quiet.`, `The frequent breaks are actually the key to the method's success.`], a: 0, e: `介紹完方法名稱後，(A) 先說「基本概念很簡單」，接著下一句才具體說明 25 分鐘＋5 分鐘的步驟，銜接最自然。(C) 談深夜讀書，與番茄鐘無關；(D) 的 The frequent breaks 必須在前文提過休息之後才能出現。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-05', d: 2, pid: 's2', q: `第 2 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`The basic idea is surprisingly easy to follow.`, `For example, a student may discover that a report she expected to finish in one round actually takes four.`, `Many people prefer to study late at night when the house is quiet.`, `The frequent breaks are actually the key to the method's success.`], a: 3, e: `第二段開始說明好處，(D)「頻繁的休息其實正是此法成功的關鍵」當主題句，下一句「知道快休息了，更容易抵抗干擾」說明休息為何有效，再下一句用 also 帶出第二個好處。(D) 的 The frequent breaks 指第一段提過的休息，所以不能放第 1 格。(B) 的 For example 必須接在「看清任務耗時」之後，不能放這裡。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-06', d: 2, pid: 's2', q: `第 3 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`The basic idea is surprisingly easy to follow.`, `For example, a report may take four rounds instead of one.`, `Many people prefer to study late at night when the house is quiet.`, `The frequent breaks are actually the key to the method's success.`], a: 1, e: `前句說這個方法能讓人看清任務實際花多久，(B) 舉報告實際要四輪而非一輪的例子，接著才能總結學會更實際地規劃。(A) 介紹基本概念、(C) 討論深夜讀書、(D) 討論休息，都沒有舉出估算耗時的例子。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-07', d: 2, pid: 's3', q: `第 1 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`In big cities, it is common to find two or three of them on the same block.`, `Most of them stay open twenty-four hours a day, so these services are available even at midnight.`, `As a result, many of them have stopped selling snacks and drinks.`, `On cold winter nights, a hot tea egg or a bowl of oden bought at the counter and enjoyed at one of these tables can be a small comfort.`], a: 0, e: `後一句說「真正讓外國人驚訝的不是它們的數量」，所以前面必須先提到「數量多」，(A) 一個街區兩三家正好鋪陳。(C) As a result 找不到因，且與後文提供多種服務矛盾。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-08', d: 2, pid: 's3', q: `第 2 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`In big cities, it is common to find two or three of them on the same block.`, `Most of them stay open twenty-four hours a day, so these services are available even at midnight.`, `As a result, many of them have stopped selling snacks and drinks.`, `On cold winter nights, a hot tea egg or a bowl of oden bought at the counter and enjoyed at one of these tables can be a small comfort.`], a: 1, e: `(B) 的 these services 指前句列出的繳費、取貨、列印、買車票；後句 This is especially helpful for people who work late「對晚下班的人特別方便」正好呼應 24 小時營業。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-09', d: 2, pid: 's3', q: `第 3 格最適合填入哪一句？`, o: [`In big cities, it is common to find two or three of them on the same block.`, `Eating is forbidden in every seating area, so office workers must always have lunch outside.`, `As a result, many of them have stopped selling snacks and drinks.`, `On winter nights, a hot tea egg enjoyed at these tables can be a comfort.`], a: 3, e: `前句介紹座位區，(D) 的 these tables 承接其中的桌子，並以冬夜在店內吃熱茶葉蛋的舒適感，帶出末句「像一位從不睡覺的鄰居」。(A) 回到店數，未承接座位區的生活用途；(B) 宣稱座位區一律禁食，直接違反前句職員在此吃午餐的敘述；(C) 把提供座位誤接成停止販售點心飲料，沒有此因果依據。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-10', d: 1, pid: 's4', q: `第 1 格最適合填入？`, o: [`faintest`, `smallest`, `brightest`, `coldest`], a: 2, e: `光害讓夜空難以看清，只剩「最亮的」幾顆星看得到，brightest 正確。faintest 最暗淡的星最先被光害掩蓋，邏輯相反；smallest、coldest 與能否看見無關。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-11', d: 1, pid: 's4', q: `第 2 格最適合填入？`, o: [`protects`, `benefits`, `ignores`, `disturbs`], a: 3, e: `後面舉例小海龜被城市燈光誤導方向，說明人造光「干擾」野生動物，disturbs 正確。protects、benefits 與例子相反；ignores 忽視，主詞是光，不合語意。` },
      { id: 'en-struct-12', d: 2, pid: 's4', q: `第 3 格最適合填入？`, o: [`harmful`, `common`, `hopeless`, `visible`], a: 2, e: `Fortunately 後接好消息，再下一句說遮光燈具與關燈就能大幅改善，所以問題「並非無解」= not hopeless。not harmful 與前文危害矛盾；not common 與開頭 growing problem 矛盾；visible 不合語意。` }
    ]
  },

  /* ───────────────────────── 7. 閱讀測驗 ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-read',
    name: '閱讀測驗',
    notes: [
      { t: '主旨題', b: '讀首段、末段與每段第一句。正確主旨要「涵蓋全文」：只講一段的細節太窄，超出文章範圍的太廣，都是陷阱。' },
      { t: '細節題', b: '用題目關鍵字回原文定位；正確答案常是原文的<b>同義改寫</b>（paraphrase），一字不差照抄原文的選項反而要小心。NOT / EXCEPT 題逐項刪去。' },
      { t: '推論題', b: '答案原文沒有直說，但「一定」能由原文推出。含 always、all、never、only 等絕對字眼，或把「可能」說成「已經」的選項，多半是過度推論。' },
      { t: '字義題與作者態度', b: '字義題：把選項代回原句，看上下文是否通順，不要只靠字典第一個意思。<br>態度題：注意評價字（beautiful, problem, fortunately）；常見選項 critical 批判、indifferent 漠不關心、appreciative 欣賞、skeptical 懷疑、objective 客觀。' },
      { t: '作者為何提到 X', b: '「Why does the author mention…?」問的是功能：舉例說明、對比、支持論點或引出下文。先找 X 所在段落的主旨句，答案通常就是「為了說明該主旨」。' }
    ],
    passages: {
      r1: `If you were asked to name the toughest animal on Earth, you might think of a lion or a shark. Many biologists, however, would choose a creature smaller than a grain of sand: the tardigrade. Also known as "water bears" because of their round bodies and slow, clumsy walk, tardigrades have eight legs and usually measure less than a millimeter long. They live almost everywhere — in moss, in deep-sea mud, and even in the ice of high mountains.<br><br>What makes tardigrades famous is their ability to survive conditions that would kill nearly any other animal. When their environment dries out, they pull in their legs, lose almost all the water in their bodies, and curl into a tiny ball called a "tun." In this state, their metabolism nearly stops. A tun can survive freezing cold, intense heat, and high levels of radiation. When water returns, the tardigrade swells up and carries on as if nothing had happened.<br><br>In 2007, researchers even attached dried tardigrades to the outside of a satellite and exposed them to outer space. After the satellite returned, some of the animals came back to life and later produced healthy young.<br><br>Scientists hope that studying these tiny survivors will lead to practical benefits. For example, if we could understand how tardigrades protect their cells while dry, we might find better ways to store vaccines without refrigeration.`,
      r2: `In many homes, a cracked bowl is simply thrown away. In Japan, however, there is a traditional craft that treats broken pottery very differently. It is called kintsugi, which means "golden joinery." Instead of hiding the damage, kintsugi artists join the broken pieces with a special lacquer made from tree sap and then cover the joins with powdered gold. The finished piece is covered with shining lines that show exactly where it once broke.<br><br>To people used to mass-produced goods, this might seem strange. Why would anyone draw attention to a flaw? The answer lies in a way of thinking that values imperfection and the passing of time. From this point of view, the cracks are not something to be ashamed of; they are part of the object's history. A repaired bowl is not less valuable than a new one — in some cases, collectors consider it more beautiful.<br><br>Kintsugi has recently become popular far beyond Japan. Workshops in Europe and North America teach the technique, and psychologists sometimes use it as a metaphor when talking with patients who have gone through hard times. Just as a bowl can be mended and become even more striking, they suggest, people can recover from painful experiences without pretending those experiences never happened.<br><br>Of course, true kintsugi is slow and costly. The lacquer can take weeks to harden, and each layer must be applied by hand. Perhaps that patience is part of the lesson, too.`,
      r3: `For decades, cities have dealt with heavy rain in the same way: cover the ground with concrete, build large drains, and send the water away as quickly as possible. As storms grow stronger, however, this approach is failing. When rain falls faster than pipes can carry it, streets turn into rivers within minutes.<br><br>A different idea is gaining support among urban planners: the "sponge city." Rather than pushing water away, a sponge city is designed to soak it up, store it, and release it slowly. Parking lots are paved with permeable materials that let rain pass through to the soil below. Rooftops are planted with grass and shrubs. Low-lying parks double as temporary ponds during storms and return to being playgrounds when the weather clears.<br><br>Supporters say the benefits go beyond flood control. Water stored underground can be used during dry seasons, and plants help cool neighborhoods on hot summer days. Green spaces also give residents more places to relax.<br><br>Critics, though, warn that a sponge city is not a magic solution. Building one requires space, money, and long-term maintenance; permeable pavement, for instance, can become blocked with dirt if it is not cleaned regularly. Moreover, in an extremely powerful storm, even the best-designed sponge city cannot absorb everything. Most experts therefore see these designs as a complement to traditional drains rather than a replacement for them.`,
      r4: `Today, QR codes are everywhere — on restaurant menus, train tickets, and payment counters. Few people know, however, that these black-and-white squares were first created not for shoppers but for factory workers.<br><br>In the early 1990s, engineers at Denso Wave, then part of a Japanese company that made parts for cars, faced a problem. Workers had to scan many traditional barcodes to keep track of parts, and each barcode could hold only about twenty characters. The team, led by engineer Masahiro Hara, set out to design a code that could store far more information and be read much faster. The result, the QR code, was released in 1994. Hara has said that the idea of using black and white squares on a grid came to him while playing the board game Go.<br><br>The team also added three large squares in the corners. These markers allow a scanner to locate the code and read it from any angle, which is why you do not need to line up your phone carefully. In addition, a QR code can often still be read even if part of it is dirty or damaged.<br><br>Perhaps the most important decision, though, was not a technical one. Denso Wave chose not to enforce its patent, allowing anyone to use the technology for free. As a result, the codes spread widely, and when smartphones with cameras became common, QR codes found a second life far beyond the factory floor.`,
      r5: `In the 1970s, Wangari Maathai noticed that something was wrong in the Kenyan countryside where she had grown up. Streams that she remembered as clear were drying up, and women had to walk farther and farther to collect firewood. The cause, she realized, was the widespread cutting of trees.<br><br>Maathai, who had become the first woman in East and Central Africa to earn a doctorate, could have stayed in her university office. Instead, she came up with a simple solution: plant trees. In 1977 she founded the Green Belt Movement, which paid rural women a small amount for every seedling they planted that survived. The idea worked on several levels. The trees protected the soil and water and provided fuel, while the women gained an income and a new sense of their own power.<br><br>Her work was not always welcomed. Because she also spoke out against corruption and against government plans to give away public land, she was arrested several times and was once beaten by police. Yet she refused to be silenced. To her, protecting the environment, fighting poverty, and defending democracy were all part of the same struggle.<br><br>In 2004, Maathai became the first African woman to receive the Nobel Peace Prize. By the time she died in 2011, the movement she started had planted tens of millions of trees. Her story shows that a single person, starting with a single seedling, can change a nation.`
    },
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-read-01', d: 1, pid: 'r1', q: `What is the passage mainly about?`, o: [`Why tardigrades are dangerous to humans`, `How a tiny animal survives extreme conditions`, `The history of space experiments with animals`, `Where scientists store vaccines`], a: 1, e: `全文從「最強韌的動物」開場，接著說明水熊蟲脫水成 tun 以抵抗極端環境、太空實驗與應用，主軸是「微小動物如何在極端環境存活」。太空實驗只是其中一段（太窄）；文中沒說牠對人類危險。` },
      { id: 'en-read-02', d: 2, pid: 'r1', q: `According to the passage, what happens when a tardigrade becomes a "tun"?`, o: [`It takes in water, expands, and stores food for later.`, `It lays its eggs, uses up its food supply, and dies.`, `It loses water, and its metabolism nearly stops.`, `It moves into deep-sea mud and feeds on tiny plants.`], a: 2, e: `第二段說水熊蟲形成 tun 時，會失去體內幾乎全部的水，代謝也幾乎停止，所以 (C) 正確。(A) 的吸水膨脹與形成 tun 相反；(B) 的產卵後死亡、(D) 的移往深海泥中覓食，都不是文中所述的 tun 變化。` },
      { id: 'en-read-03', d: 1, pid: 'r1', q: `The phrase "carries on" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to ＿＿＿.`, o: [`continues`, `complains`, `hides`, `travels`], a: 0, e: `水回來後，水熊蟲膨脹並 carries on as if nothing had happened「若無其事地繼續過活」，carry on = continue。其他選項代回句中都不通順。` },
      { id: 'en-read-04', d: 3, pid: 'r1', q: `What can be inferred from the last paragraph?`, o: [`Tardigrades are already used to make vaccines.`, `Some vaccines currently need to be kept cold.`, `Tardigrades cannot survive without refrigeration.`, `Scientists have stopped studying tardigrades in space.`], a: 1, e: `作者希望找到「不需冷藏就能保存疫苗」的方法，可推知目前有些疫苗需要冷藏。(A) 把「可能」說成「已經」，屬過度推論；(C) 與文意無關；(D) 文中未提。` },
      { id: 'en-read-05', d: 2, pid: 'r2', q: `Which of the following best describes kintsugi?`, o: [`A way of making new bowls look old`, `A technique for producing identical pottery in large quantities`, `A type of glue made entirely of gold`, `A method of repairing pottery that highlights the cracks`], a: 3, e: `第一段說金繼用樹漆黏合破片，再把金粉覆在接縫上，保留閃亮的裂痕，因此 (D) 的凸顯裂痕修復法正確。(A) 不是把新碗做舊；(B) 的大量製造相同陶器不是這種修復工藝；(C) 也誤把樹漆與表面的金粉當成純金膠。` },
      { id: 'en-read-06', d: 2, pid: 'r2', q: `According to the passage, why might some people find kintsugi strange?`, o: [`It makes damage more noticeable instead of hiding it.`, `It is much cheaper than buying a new bowl.`, `It replaces traditional tree sap with factory-made chemicals.`, `It is not allowed to be practiced outside Japan.`], a: 0, e: `第二段問「為什麼要讓缺陷引人注目」，說明一些人覺得奇怪的是金繼會凸顯而非隱藏損傷，選 (A)。(B) 的價格比較沒有依據，文章只形容工藝慢而昂貴；(C) 與第一段使用樹漆相反；(D) 與歐美也有工作坊的敘述相反。` },
      { id: 'en-read-07', d: 3, pid: 'r2', q: `Why does the author mention psychologists in the third paragraph?`, o: [`To show that kintsugi can cure mental illness`, `To explain how kintsugi lacquer is made`, `To link kintsugi with recovery from hardship`, `To criticize the commercialization of kintsugi`], a: 2, e: `第三段說心理師以金繼作為隱喻，說明人能從痛苦經驗中復原，同時承認那些經驗，故 (C) 正確。(A) 把隱喻誇大成治癒疾病；(B) 的樹漆製法、(D) 的商業化批評，都不是該段提到心理師的目的。` },
      { id: 'en-read-08', d: 2, pid: 'r2', q: `What is the author's attitude toward kintsugi?`, o: [`Disapproving`, `Appreciative`, `Indifferent`, `Doubtful`], a: 1, e: `作者介紹金繼的理念，提到修復後可能更美，最後也肯定耐心的價值，態度是 appreciative（欣賞的）。提到慢且昂貴是在說明工藝特性，整體並非 disapproving（不贊同的）、indifferent（漠不關心的）或 doubtful（懷疑的）。` },
      { id: 'en-read-09', d: 2, pid: 'r3', q: `What is the main idea of the passage?`, o: [`Concrete is still the best material for modern cities.`, `Traditional drains should be removed from all cities.`, `Sponge cities help manage rainwater but have limits.`, `Parks are more important than playgrounds.`], a: 2, e: `(A) 文中沒有評定混凝土是否為最佳城市建材，反而指出以混凝土鋪地並迅速排水的做法面臨困難，故不是主旨。(B) 末段說海綿設計應補充傳統排水，而非拆除全部排水設施。(C) 全文介紹海綿城市管理雨水的優點，也說明空間、成本、維護及極端暴雨下的限制，完整涵蓋主旨，正確。(D) 公園雨後可恢復遊樂用途，但文中未比較公園與遊樂場的重要性。` },
      { id: 'en-read-10', d: 1, pid: 'r3', q: `Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature of a sponge city?`, o: [`Parking lots paved with permeable materials`, `Rooftops planted with grass and shrubs`, `Low-lying parks used as ponds during storms`, `Tunnels that pump rainwater into the sea`], a: 3, e: `題目問「未提到」的設計。第二段列出透水停車場、屋頂種植草與灌木、低窪公園在暴雨時兼作池塘，(A)(B)(C) 都有依據；(D) 把雨水抽往海裡的隧道則沒有在文中列為海綿城市的特色。` },
      { id: 'en-read-11', d: 2, pid: 'r3', q: `The phrase "double as" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to ＿＿＿.`, o: [`also serve as`, `become twice as large as`, `are replaced by`, `look similar to`], a: 0, e: `公園在暴雨時「兼作」臨時池塘，天晴後又恢復為遊樂場，double as = 同時也當作。double 在這裡不是「變兩倍」，選 (B) 是望文生義。` },
      { id: 'en-read-12', d: 3, pid: 'r3', q: `Which statement would the experts mentioned at the end most likely agree with?`, o: [`Cities should stop building drains altogether.`, `Sponge-city design should complement conventional drains.`, `Permeable pavement stays effective without any regular cleaning.`, `A well-designed sponge city can prevent all floods.`], a: 1, e: `末句明說海綿設計是傳統排水的 complement（補充），而非 replacement（取代），故 (B) 正確。(A) 主張完全停建排水，與此相反；(C) 忽略透水鋪面需定期清理；(D) 也違反再好設計都無法吸收極端暴雨全部雨水的限制。` },
      { id: 'en-read-13', d: 1, pid: 'r4', q: `According to the passage, QR codes were originally developed to ＿＿＿.`, o: [`keep track of car parts in factories`, `help restaurant customers pay for meals`, `teach people the game of Go`, `replace paper train tickets`], a: 0, e: `第一、二段說 QR 碼最早是為工廠工人開發，用來追蹤汽車零件，故 (A) 正確。(B) 的餐廳付款及 (D) 的車票不屬原始開發目的；(C) 的圍棋是設計靈感來源，並不是要用 QR 碼教人下棋。` },
      { id: 'en-read-14', d: 2, pid: 'r4', q: `What is the purpose of the three large squares in the corners of a QR code?`, o: [`To copy the arrangement of pieces on a Go board`, `To store the company's name`, `To show scanners the code's position and angle`, `To keep the code from getting dirty`], a: 2, e: `第三段說三個大方塊讓掃描器找到碼的位置，並能從任何角度讀取，故 (C) 正確。(A) 混淆圍棋提供的黑白格靈感；(B) 的公司名稱不是定位標記的用途；(D) 混淆了另述的部分髒汙仍可讀取能力，標記本身不會防止髒汙。` },
      { id: 'en-read-15', d: 3, pid: 'r4', q: `According to the last paragraph, what helped QR codes spread so widely?`, o: [`Traditional barcodes were banned by law.`, `Denso Wave developed the cameras used in smartphones.`, `Factory workers refused to use barcodes.`, `Denso Wave let anyone use QR codes free of charge.`], a: 3, e: `末段說 Denso Wave 選擇不行使專利，讓任何人免費使用，後接 As a result 說碼因而廣泛流傳，所以 (D) 正確。(B) 的相機開發不是文中所述的公司決策；(A) 的法律禁用與 (C) 的工人拒用，也沒有出現在文中。` },
      { id: 'en-read-16', d: 1, pid: 'r5', q: `What problem did Maathai first notice in the Kenyan countryside?`, o: [`Girls in rural villages were denied access to school.`, `Too many trees were growing near villages.`, `Streams were drying up, and firewood was harder to find.`, `Universities lacked female professors.`], a: 2, e: `(A) 女孩被拒絕就學不是第一段所述問題，文中也未提及。(B) 大量砍樹造成樹木減少，不是樹木太多。(C) 第一段說溪流正逐漸乾涸，婦女必須走更遠才能取得柴火，與此項一致，因此正確。(D) 大學缺少女教授不是她在鄉間首先注意到的問題，文中也未這樣敘述。` },
      { id: 'en-read-17', d: 1, pid: 'r5', q: `How did the Green Belt Movement encourage women to plant trees?`, o: [`It paid them for each seedling that survived.`, `It required them by law to plant trees.`, `It offered them university degrees.`, `It gave each woman a piece of land for planting trees.`], a: 0, e: `第二段說運動為婦女種下且存活的每株樹苗支付少量報酬，這就是鼓勵植樹的方式，選 (A)。報酬有「樹苗存活」的條件；(B) 法律強迫、(C) 贈予學位、(D) 分給每位婦女種樹土地，都不是文中所說的獎勵。` },
      { id: 'en-read-18', d: 3, pid: 'r5', q: `Which of the following best expresses Maathai's view described in the third paragraph?`, o: [`Environmental issues should be kept separate from politics.`, `Environmental, economic, and political struggles are linked.`, `Peaceful protest is useless against a powerful government.`, `Only scientists should make environmental decisions.`], a: 1, e: `第三段末把保護環境、對抗貧窮、捍衛民主稱為同一場奮鬥，正是 (B) 所說環境、經濟與政治層面的奮鬥相連。(A) 主張環境與政治分開，與她的觀點相反；(C) 的和平抗議無用、(D) 的只由科學家決定，也都不是文中觀點。` },
      { id: 'en-read-19', d: 2, pid: 'r5', q: `Which word best describes Wangari Maathai as presented in the passage?`, o: [`Timid`, `Indifferent`, `Careless`, `Persistent`], a: 3, e: `(A) Timid 是膽怯的；她多次被捕、遭毆打仍勇於發聲，不符文中形象。(B) Indifferent 是漠不關心的；她積極投入環境、貧困與民主議題，與此相反。(C) Careless 是粗心的；文中沒有描寫她行事粗心，無法據此選答。(D) Persistent 是堅持不懈的；她遭打壓仍不肯沉默，持續推動植樹多年，因此最符合文中形象。` }
    ]
  },

  /* ───────────────────────── 8. 混合題與圖表閱讀 ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-mixed',
    name: '混合題與圖表閱讀',
    notes: [
      { t: '圖表三步驟', b: '①先看標題、單位與欄位名稱 ②讀題目，確認要找哪一欄 ③回表格取數字。計算題先列式再算，別心算跳步。' },
      { t: '公告與廣告的陷阱字', b: 'except（除了）、only（僅限）、at least（至少）、in advance（事先）、cannot be combined（不得合併使用）、valid（有效的）。條件與限制最常出題。' },
      { t: '多選題策略', b: '每個選項當成獨立的是非題，各自回原文找證據；數字型敘述一定要實際算一次。' },
      { t: '百分比與倍數', b: '百分比 = 部分 ÷ 全體 × 100%。「打九折」英文是 10% off；twice as long as = 兩倍長。' }
    ],
    passages: {
      m1: `<b>Riverside Public Library — Summer Notice</b><br>Opening hours (July 1 – August 31):<br>• Monday: Closed<br>• Tuesday – Friday: 9:00 a.m. – 8:00 p.m.<br>• Saturday – Sunday: 10:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m.<br><br><b>Borrowing rules</b><br>• Books: up to 10 items for 21 days<br>• DVDs: up to 3 items for 7 days<br>• Late fees: NT$5 per book per day; NT$10 per DVD per day<br><br><b>Summer Reading Challenge</b> (for students aged 12–18)<br>Read 8 books by August 20 and receive a free tote bag. Register at the front desk with your student ID.<br><br><i>Note: The library will be closed on August 15 for system maintenance.</i>`,
      m2: `The table below shows the results of a survey of 400 students at Seaside High School about how they usually get to school.<br><br><b>Method — Number of students — Average travel time</b><br>Walking — 60 — 12 minutes<br>Bicycle — 100 — 18 minutes<br>Bus — 140 — 35 minutes<br>MRT — 40 — 25 minutes<br>Driven by parents — 60 — 20 minutes`,
      m3: `<b>SUNSHINE COOKING STUDIO — Learn to Cook Taiwanese Classics!</b><br>Course A: Beef Noodle Soup — Saturdays 2:00–5:00 p.m. — NT$1,800<br>Course B: Pineapple Cakes — Sundays 10:00 a.m.–1:00 p.m. — NT$1,200<br>Course C: Night Market Snacks (3 dishes) — Fridays 7:00–9:30 p.m. — NT$1,500<br><br>• Sign up for any two courses and get 10% off the total price.<br>• Students with a valid student ID pay NT$200 less for a single course. (This cannot be combined with the two-course discount.)<br>• All ingredients and aprons are provided. Please bring a container to take your food home.<br>• Each class is limited to 12 people. Please book at least 3 days in advance.`
    },
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-mixed-01', d: 1, pid: 'm1', q: `If you want to visit the library on a Monday evening in July, what will happen?`, o: [`The library will close at 5:00 p.m.`, `You can stay until 8:00 p.m.`, `The library will be closed all day.`, `You must show your student ID to enter.`], a: 2, e: `公告寫明 Monday: Closed，七月週一整天休館。(A) 是週末的閉館時間、(B) 是週二至週五；學生證只在報名閱讀挑戰時需要。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-02', d: 2, pid: 'm1', q: `Amy returned 2 books 3 days late and 1 DVD 2 days late. How much does she have to pay in late fees?`, o: [`NT$30`, `NT$35`, `NT$40`, `NT$50`], a: 3, e: `書：2 本 × 3 天 × NT$5 = NT$30；DVD：1 片 × 2 天 × NT$10 = NT$20；合計 NT$50。(A) NT$30 只算了書，漏掉 DVD。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-03', d: 2, pid: 'm1', q: `Who will receive a free tote bag from the Summer Reading Challenge?`, o: [`A 10-year-old who registers with her student ID and reads 12 books by August 18`, `A 16-year-old who registers with her student ID and reads 8 books by August 18`, `A 20-year-old who registers with his student ID and reads 15 books by August 18`, `A 15-year-old who registers with his student ID and finishes 8 books on August 25`], a: 1, e: `須為12–18歲學生、持學生證登記，並最遲於8月20日讀完8本書；by August 20 包含8月20日當天。(A) 10歲，低於年齡下限，不能領取。(B) 16歲、持學生證登記，且最遲8月18日已讀完8本，符合全部條件，正確。(C) 20歲，超過年齡上限。(D) 8月25日才讀完，已超過截止日。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-04', d: 3, type: 'multi', pid: 'm1', q: `According to the notice, which of the following statements are true?（多選）`, o: [`On Wednesdays in July, the library is open for 11 hours.`, `DVDs can be borrowed for one-third as long as books.`, `The library will be closed on August 15 for system maintenance.`, `The library opens earlier on weekends than on Tuesdays through Fridays.`, `The daily late fee for a DVD is higher than that for a book.`], a: [0,1,2,4], e: `(A) 週三09:00–20:00共11小時，正確。(B) DVD可借7天，書可借21天，7是21的三分之一，正確。(C) 公告明說8月15日因系統維護休館，正確。(D) 週末10:00開門，週二至週五09:00開門；週末較晚，不是較早，錯誤。(E) DVD每天逾期費NT$10，書為NT$5，DVD較高，正確。因此正確項恰為(A)(B)(C)(E)，a為[0,1,2,4]。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-05', d: 1, pid: 'm2', q: `What is the most common way for students at Seaside High School to get to school?`, o: [`Bicycle`, `Bus`, `MRT`, `Walking`], a: 1, e: `人數最多的是 Bus（140 人）。Bicycle 100 人次之；注意題目問「人數最多」，不是「最快」或「最慢」。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-06', d: 2, pid: 'm2', q: `What percentage of the surveyed students ride bicycles to school?`, o: [`15%`, `20%`, `25%`, `35%`], a: 2, e: `100 ÷ 400 × 100% = 25%。15% 是步行或家長接送（60 ÷ 400）、35% 是公車（140 ÷ 400），都是看錯列的干擾項。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-07', d: 2, pid: 'm2', q: `Which statement is supported by the table?`, o: [`Students who walk have the shortest average travel time.`, `More students take the MRT than ride bicycles.`, `Being driven by parents is the fastest way to get to school.`, `Half of the students take the bus.`], a: 0, e: `步行平均 12 分鐘，是所有方式中最短，(A) 正確。(B) 捷運 40 人少於腳踏車 100 人；(C) 家長接送 20 分鐘，比步行慢；(D) 公車 140 人只佔 35%，不到一半。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-08', d: 3, pid: 'm2', q: `Which of the following can NOT be determined from the table?`, o: [`Why many students prefer taking the bus`, `The total number of students surveyed`, `How long bus riders spend getting to school on average`, `How many students walk to school`], a: 0, e: `表格只有人數與平均時間，沒有任何關於「原因」的資料，所以無法判斷學生偏好公車的理由。總人數 400、公車平均 35 分鐘、步行 60 人都能直接從表格讀出。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-09', d: 2, pid: 'm3', q: `Kevin, who is not a student, signs up for Course A and Course C. How much will he pay in total?`, o: [`NT$3,300`, `NT$3,100`, `NT$2,900`, `NT$2,970`], a: 3, e: `兩門課原價 1,800 + 1,500 = 3,300 元，報兩門打九折（10% off）：3,300 × 0.9 = 2,970 元。(A) 忘了折扣；(C) 是誤把每門課減 200 元的學生優惠套用上去。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-10', d: 1, pid: 'm3', q: `Which course would suit someone who is free only on weekday evenings?`, o: [`Course A`, `Course C`, `Course B`, `None of the courses`], a: 1, e: `Course C 在週五晚上 7:00–9:30，週五是平日（weekday），且在晚上。Course A 是週六下午、Course B 是週日上午，都在週末。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-11', d: 1, pid: 'm3', q: `What should participants bring to class?`, o: [`An apron`, `Their own ingredients`, `A container`, `A recipe book`], a: 2, e: `廣告寫 Please bring a container to take your food home。材料與圍裙都由教室提供（All ingredients and aprons are provided），食譜書未提及。` },
      { id: 'en-mixed-12', d: 3, pid: 'm3', q: `Mia, a high school student with a valid student ID, wants to take only Course B. Which of the following is true?`, o: [`She will pay NT$1,000.`, `She can sign up on the morning of the class.`, `She will get 10% off the price.`, `She has to bring her own apron.`], a: 0, e: `只報一門課可用學生優惠減 200 元：1,200 − 200 = 1,000 元，(A) 正確。(B) 需至少 3 天前預約；(C) 九折只適用報兩門課；(D) 圍裙由教室提供。` }
    ]
  },

  /* ───────────────────────── 9. 中譯英與寫作句型 ───────────────────────── */
  {
    id: 'en-trans',
    name: '中譯英與寫作句型',
    notes: [
      { t: '「花時間」三句型', b: 'S + spend + 時間 + (in) V-ing；It takes + 人 + 時間 + to V；S + pay + 錢 + for + 物。spend 不接 to V，pay 不接時間。' },
      { t: '後面接 V-ing 的高頻句型', b: 'look forward to V-ing、be worth V-ing、be used to V-ing（習慣於）、devote oneself to V-ing、object to V-ing。這裡的 to 是<b>介系詞</b>，不是不定詞。' },
      { t: '對稱與比較句型', b: 'not only A but also B（A、B 詞性相同）；would rather A than B（A、B 都是原形）；so + adj. + that…；too + adj. + to V = so + adj. + that…can\'t…；It is not until A that B（直到 A 才 B）。' },
      { t: '主詞動詞一致', b: '<b>The number of</b> + 複數名詞 + <b>單數動詞</b>（…的數量）；<b>A number of</b> + 複數名詞 + <b>複數動詞</b>（許多…）。' },
      { t: '懸垂分詞', b: '分詞片語的邏輯主詞必須等於主要子句主詞：Walking along the beach, <b>I</b> found the sunset beautiful.（不是 the sunset walking）。主詞不同時要保留主詞：It being a rainy day, …' }
    ],
    questions: [
      { id: 'en-trans-01', d: 1, q: `「我昨天花了兩個小時寫作業。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`I spent two hours to do my homework yesterday.`, `I spent two hours doing my homework yesterday.`, `It spent me two hours doing my homework yesterday.`, `I paid two hours on doing my homework yesterday.`], a: 1, e: `人 + spend + 時間 + V-ing，(B) 正確。spend 後不接 to V（A 錯）；虛主詞句型要用 It took me two hours to do…（C 錯）；pay 用於金錢，不用於時間（D 錯）。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-02', d: 2, q: `「直到媽媽回家，我才開始做晚餐。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`Until my mother came home, I started cooking dinner.`, `I started cooking dinner until my mother came home.`, `I didn't start cooking dinner before my mother didn't come home.`, `I didn't start cooking dinner until my mother came home.`], a: 3, e: `「直到…才…」= not…until…，主句要用否定：I didn't start…until…。(A)(B) 少了否定，意思變成「一直在做晚餐直到媽媽回家」，且 start 是瞬間動詞不能與 until 連用表持續；(C) 雙重否定語意錯亂。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-03', d: 2, q: `「這本書值得一讀再讀。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`This book is worth to read again and again.`, `This book is worthy reading again and again.`, `This book is worth reading again and again.`, `This book worths reading again and again.`], a: 2, e: `be worth + V-ing（主動形式表被動意義），(C) 正確。worth 後不接不定詞（A 錯）；worthy 要接 of：be worthy of being read（B 錯）；worth 是形容詞／介系詞性質，不是動詞，不能加 s（D 錯）。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-04', d: 2, q: `「你越早出發，就越早到達。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`The earlier you leave, the sooner you will arrive.`, `Earlier you leave, sooner you arrive.`, `The more early you leave, the more soon you will arrive.`, `The earlier you will leave, the sooner you arrive.`], a: 0, e: `The 比較級 + S + V, the 比較級 + S + V，(A) 正確。(B) 少了 the；(C) early、soon 的比較級是 earlier、sooner，不用 more；(D) 前半是條件，應以現在式代替未來式，will 放錯位置。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-05', d: 3, q: `「要不是有你的鼓勵，我早就放棄了。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`If it were not for your encouragement, I would give up long ago.`, `Without your encouragement, I will have given up long ago.`, `Had it not been for your encouragement, I would have given up long ago.`, `If it had not been for your encouragement, I would give up long ago.`], a: 2, e: `「早就放棄了」是與過去事實相反，用 If it had not been for… = Had it not been for…，主句 would have p.p.，(C) 正確。(A)(D) 主句 would give up 搭配 long ago 時態不合；(B) will have given up 不是假設語氣。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-06', d: 2, q: `「他不但會說英文，也會說日文。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`He speaks not only English but also Japanese.`, `Not only he speaks English but also Japanese.`, `He not only speaks English but also speaking Japanese.`, `He speaks both English as well as Japanese.`], a: 0, e: `not only A but also B 要對稱，A、B 都是名詞（English / Japanese），(A) 正確。(B) Not only 放句首必須倒裝（Not only does he speak…）；(C) not only 後接動詞 speaks，but also 後也必須是同形式的動詞 speaks，speaking 破壞對稱；(D) both…and 與 as well as 混用。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-07', d: 2, q: `下列哪一句文法正確？`, o: [`The number of tourists have increased rapidly.`, `A number of tourists has visited the temple.`, `The number of tourists has increased rapidly.`, `The numbers of tourist has increased rapidly.`], a: 2, e: `The number of + 複數名詞，主詞是 number（數量），動詞用單數 has，(C) 正確。(A) 動詞應為單數；(B) A number of（許多）後動詞應為複數 have；(D) of 後應接複數 tourists。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-08', d: 3, q: `「Being a rainy day, the picnic was canceled.」這句有文法錯誤，最適當的修正是？`, o: [`Being rainy day, the picnic was canceled.`, `It being a rainy day, the picnic was canceled.`, `Been a rainy day, the picnic was canceled.`, `Being a rainy day, we canceled the picnic.`], a: 1, e: `原句懸垂分詞：野餐不可能「是雨天」。兩子句主詞不同，要保留主詞 It 成為獨立分詞構句：It being a rainy day, …，(B) 正確。(D) 變成「我們是雨天」，仍是懸垂分詞；(A) 少冠詞；(C) Been 不能單獨引導分詞構句。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-09', d: 1, q: `「我很期待見到你。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`I am looking forward to see you.`, `I am looking forward for seeing you.`, `I am looking forward seeing you.`, `I am looking forward to seeing you.`], a: 3, e: `look forward to 的 to 是介系詞，後接 V-ing，(D) 正確。(A) 誤把 to 當不定詞；(B) 介系詞應為 to；(C) 漏掉 to。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-10', d: 2, q: `「這部電影非常感人，以至於很多觀眾都哭了。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`The movie was such touching that many viewers cried.`, `The movie was so touched that many viewers cried.`, `The movie was so touching that many viewers cried.`, `The movie was too touching that many viewers cried.`], a: 2, e: `so + adj. + that…，電影「令人感動」用 touching，(C) 正確。such 後要接名詞（such a touching movie）；touched 是「人感到感動」；too…to 不能接 that 子句。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-11', d: 3, q: `「人們直到失去健康，才知道健康的可貴。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`Not until people lose their health they realize its value.`, `It is not until people lose their health that they realize its value.`, `It is not until people lose their health when they realize its value.`, `It is until people lose their health that they don't realize its value.`], a: 1, e: `It is not until A that B 是「直到 A 才 B」的強調句型，(B) 正確。(A) Not until 放句首，主要子句須倒裝（do they realize）；(C) 強調句要用 that 而非 when；(D) not 的位置錯誤，語意也變了。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-12', d: 2, q: `下列哪一句的意思與「Tom is too young to drive a car.」相同？`, o: [`Tom is young enough to drive a car.`, `Tom is so young that he can drive a car.`, `Tom is not young to drive a car.`, `Tom is so young that he can't drive a car.`], a: 3, e: `too + adj. + to V（太…而不能…）= so + adj. + that + S + can't + V，(D) 正確。(A) enough to 表「夠…可以」，意思相反；(B) 少了否定；(C) 不合文法也不合語意。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-13', d: 3, q: `「Walking along the beach, the sunset looked beautiful.」這句有懸垂分詞的錯誤，最適當的修正是？`, o: [`Walking along the beach, I found the sunset beautiful.`, `Walked along the beach, the sunset looked beautiful.`, `Walking along the beach, the sunset was looked beautiful.`, `To walk along the beach, the sunset looked beautiful.`], a: 0, e: `夕陽不會在沙灘散步，分詞的邏輯主詞必須是人，把主要子句主詞改為 I，(A) 正確。(B)(D) 只改了分詞形式，主詞仍是夕陽；(C) look 是連綴動詞，不能用被動。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-14', d: 1, q: `「台灣以夜市聞名。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`Taiwan is famous as its night markets.`, `Taiwan is famous for its night markets.`, `Taiwan is famous to its night markets.`, `Taiwan famous for its night markets.`], a: 1, e: `be famous for + 特色／事物「以…聞名」，(B) 正確。be famous as 是「以某身分聞名」（Taiwan is famous as a food paradise）；famous to 不是正確搭配；(D) 缺少 be 動詞。` },
      { id: 'en-trans-15', d: 3, q: `「我寧願待在家，也不願在雨中出門。」最佳英譯為？`, o: [`I would rather stay home than going out in the rain.`, `I would rather to stay home than go out in the rain.`, `I would rather staying home than go out in the rain.`, `I would rather stay home than go out in the rain.`], a: 3, e: `would rather + 原形 + than + 原形，前後對稱，(D) 正確。(A) than 後應為原形 go；(B) would rather 後不接 to；(C) would rather 後不接 V-ing。` }
    ]
  }
]);
